Issue: We are powder coating 300 small pieces, so we need to bend S-hooks out of a conductive wire so that the powder workpiece can be charged and the powder will stick

Goal:
    1. Make a machine that will feed and bend MIG rod using a series of bends to make an S-hook, and cut off the piece once it has been entirely bent

Concept:
    1. Use a MIG wire feeder and a NEMA 17 stepper motor to feed through a channel where a NEMA 23 stepper motor makes tangential small turns to make a gradual bend per hook
    2. Put the entire bending assembly of the motor and the bending bearing on a tube with a gear meshed with another NEMA 27, such that the wire can feed through the tube while the gantry turns around the wire
    3. Make a solid base to attach the entire mechanism to with 3D modeling and 3D printing
    4. Drive the mechanism with a DLC MAX 32 Stepper Motor Controller
        A. Calibrate acceleration, max speed, and feed rates with LaserGRBL 
        B. Control with G-Code
    5. Once the hook has been finished, cut the hook off of the rest of the wire with a blade driven by a solenoid

Issues: 
    1. G-Code became overly complicated, and the time it would take to fix was not worth it for the business
    2. The solenoid was not strong enough to cut the MIG wire
        A. Either need a bigger solenoid or a sharper blade

PROJECT UNFINISHED 
